hints.content:
Notice the numerator is missing a $1^{\text{st}}$ degree term. Let's add it as $0x$.

$\begin{array}{r}
x\phantom{+1}
\\
x+4|\overline{x^2+0x+1}
\\
\mathllap{-(}\underline{x^2+4x\phantom{+1}\rlap )}
\\
{}-4x+1
\end{array}$

options.explanation:
We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x+4$ to get $x^2+k$ (no $x$-term) where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x-4$ (remember the difference of squares pattern):

$\blueD{(x+4)(x-4)=x^2-16}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2+1}{x+4}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-16})+17}{x+4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-16}}{x+4}+\dfrac{17}{x+4}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x+4)(x-4)=x^2-16}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-16}}{x+4}+\dfrac{17}{x+4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x+4)}}\blueD{(x-4)}}{\cancel{x+4}}+\dfrac{17}{x+4}
\\\\
&=x-4+\dfrac{17}{x+4}
\end{align}$
